If photonic computing continues to mature, possible applications include:

### AI Inference

* Computer vision
* Neural-network inference
* Signal classification
* Pattern recognition

### Edge AI

* Autonomous systems
* Robotics
* Sensor processing
* Real-time perception

### Data Centers

* AI acceleration
* High-bandwidth processing
* Interconnects

### Communications

* Optical signal processing
* High-speed data processing

### Scientific Computing

* Specialized mathematical operations
* Signal analysis
* Optimization

These should be considered potential applications rather than guaranteed outcomes.

---

# 28. Open Questions for Future Research

The following questions are particularly interesting for further investigation:

### Q1

Can photonic hardware efficiently run transformer-style workloads?

### Q2

Can photonic memory scale to billions or trillions of parameters?

### Q3

Can nonlinear neural-network operations be implemented optically without large electronic overhead?

### Q4

Can a photonic accelerator work alongside a GPU without creating a new communication bottleneck?

### Q5

Can sensors directly feed photonic computation without converting all information into conventional digital representations first?

### Q6

What happens when computation, memory and sensing are designed together from the beginning?

---

# 29. Conclusion

Photonic computing is not simply an attempt to make an electronic computer faster.

It represents a different way of thinking about computation.

Instead of relying exclusively on electrical signals, photonic systems use properties of light such as:

* Optical bandwidth
* Interference
* Wavelength
* Spatial parallelism
* Fast signal propagation

to perform or accelerate selected computational operations.

The field has progressed from theoretical optical computing toward integrated photonic neural networks, photonic tensor processors, neuromorphic photonic systems and photonic memory.

At the same time, important challenges remain.

Photonic computing must still address:

* Optical-electrical conversion
* Nonlinear operations
* Memory
* Optical losses
* Noise
* Manufacturing
* Software
* Scalability

Therefore, it is premature to describe photonic computing as a replacement for GPUs.

A more realistic possibility is a heterogeneous computing future in which:

```text
CPU
 +
GPU
 +
NPU
 +
Photonics
 +
Memory
 +
Sensors
```

work together.

The most interesting question may therefore not be:

> **"Can light replace the GPU?"**

but:

> **"Which parts of AI computation should be performed using light?"**

That question opens a much larger research space.

---

# 30. Key Takeaways

```text
                 PHOTONIC COMPUTING
                         │
                         ▼
                  Computing with Light
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼              ▼              ▼
      Bandwidth      Parallelism    Interference
          │              │              │
          └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                         ▼
                Optical Processing
                         │
                         ▼
                AI Acceleration
                         │
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             ▼                       ▼
      Neural Networks          In-Memory AI
             │                       │
             └───────────┬───────────┘
                         ▼
                Future Hybrid Systems
```

### One-line takeaway

> **The future of AI hardware may not be about building one faster processor; it may be about choosing the right physical medium for each part of computation.**

